Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||02:46 PM||||I read the kit car mag review of it when it was first released.  Apparantly the suspension upgrade is quite well done and could easily handle a little off-roading.  The only drawback being the obvious lack of 4WD.  <P>I still think it'd be a fun "winter" vehicle here in Minnesota  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  But not anytime soon.<P>About the chrome 20" wheels- they had a cheaper set mounted with off-road tires, they seem to fit the vehicle better.  We really should give them some credit for creating another unique vehicle instead of ripping off another Ferrari/Lambo/GT40!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.cybercars.com/jalap.gif">  <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/jalapeno.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/jalapeno.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||12-05-2001||03:22 PM||||Ahh the Jalapeno...<P>Probably the reason why most of us don't want to see Pontiac bring back the Fiero right now.<P>It's ok.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||12-05-2001||04:23 PM||||Hey, lack of 4wd doesn't mean much with a rear/mid engined car!  My baja VW will do very good offroading; never fails to impress people when I go offroading.  The fiero doesn't have quite the rear weight bias, but you could easily lighten the front and shift a little weight back, install a V8, and have a very unique 2wd off-roader.  IMO, the only thing it's lacking is a good aftermarket tall suspension setup.  The factory components like axles/cvs and suspension arms are limited, and lots of abuse would likely kill them.  CV joints (just like U joints) don't like lots of torque at extreme angles...but there are ways to get around it if somebody really wanted to.  Then again, if they really wanted a 2 seater that would do all that, if they didn't mind being non-unique they could always do a VW <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
